# Trivy gate baseline — pre-existing OS-package findings in the base images.
#
# ── What this file is NOT ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# It is not a waiver, and it hides nothing. scripts/scan-image.sh runs Trivy
# twice and applies this file to ONE of the two passes:
#
#   report pass  --ignorefile /dev/null   every FIXABLE finding, at every
#                --ignore-unfixed         severity and package type, including
#                                         everything listed here →
#                                         SARIF → GitHub code scanning.
#                                         (Unfixed findings go to the census in
#                                         the job log — see scan-image.sh. Every
#                                         entry in THIS file is fixable by
#                                         definition, so all of them still
#                                         reach code scanning.)
#
#   gate pass    --ignorefile .trivyignore  fixable HIGH/CRITICAL OS packages,
#                                           minus this list → blocks the merge.
#
# The effect is that the gate blocks *regressions*. A newly disclosed CVE, or
# one a PR introduces via a base-image change, goes red on that PR. Debt that
# arrived with a third-party base image Atlas does not build stays visible in
# code scanning without pinning every unrelated PR red.
#
# ── Why there is a baseline at all ────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Because two different things get scanned, and only one of them ships.
#
#   BUILT images (deploy/api, deploy/web, deploy/docs) — the artifacts that
#   reach Railway and customers. Their runner stages run `apt-get upgrade` /
#   `apk upgrade`, so every entry below is already patched out of them.
#   Measured 2026-08-12 with the pinned Trivy 0.72.0: ZERO gate-blocking
#   findings in all three.
#
#   BARE base images (oven/bun:1.3.13, caddy:2.11.4-alpine) — scanned by the
#   base-images tier for pin regressions and for DISCOVERY, which is the only
#   thing that notices a new Dockerfile with a base nobody enumerated. Nothing
#   has upgraded these yet at the moment they are scanned, so they still carry
#   the findings below.
#
# So the entries here are not a deferral of unfixed debt. They are the gap
# between "what the base ships with" and "what our images ship with", and the
# runner-stage upgrade is what closes it.
#
# ⚠️ An earlier draft of this change emptied the file entirely, on a
# measurement taken against the BUILT images only. CI failed both base-image
# legs immediately. Recording that because the mistake is easy to repeat: a
# green built-image scan says nothing about the bare-base gate, and the two
# numbers are not interchangeable.
#
# ⚠️ Why baselining is legitimate HERE and would not have been before: until
# 2026-08-12 no Atlas image upgraded its packages, so a bare-base CVE really did
# ship — including into the create-atlas docker templates a customer scaffolds
# from. Every runtime stage in the tree now upgrades (deploy/api, deploy/web,
# deploy/docs, packages/sandbox-sidecar, examples/docker, and all three
# create-atlas/templates/docker Dockerfiles). If a future Dockerfile adds a
# runtime stage WITHOUT an upgrade, these entries silently start lying.
#
# ── Scope note ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Only OS packages belong here, because only OS packages are gated. Library
# findings inside the images (npm, Go modules) are reported to code scanning but
# never block.
#
# ⚠️ This paragraph used to end "— they are Dependabot's remit, and it can open
# the fix PR." That is false and was already corrected in scripts/scan-image.sh
# by #4878; the correction just never reached this file. Atlas uses bun, and
# `bun` is a separate Dependabot ecosystem from `npm` with NO security updates,
# so no configuration produces a fix PR here. Remediation for library findings
# is hand-authored root `overrides` plus parent bumps. Read the SCOPE section in
# scripts/scan-image.sh for the real detection -> visibility -> remediation
# handoff, and do not restate it here — one copy, one place to keep true.
#
# ── How to re-measure ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# After ANY change to a base-image pin or a runner-stage package install,
# re-scan and delete every entry that no longer appears. Scan BOTH surfaces —
# they answer different questions and neither substitutes for the other:
#
#   the bare bases decide what belongs in THIS file (the base-images gate):
#
#     bash scripts/list-runtime-base-images.sh | while read -r i; do \
#       trivy image --scanners vuln --pkg-types os --severity HIGH,CRITICAL \
#         --ignore-unfixed --ignorefile /dev/null "$i"; done
#
#   the built images decide whether the runner-stage upgrades still work, which
#   is the premise every entry here rests on. Expect ZERO from each:
#
#     docker build -f deploy/api/Dockerfile -t probe-api . && \
#       trivy image --scanners vuln --pkg-types os --severity HIGH,CRITICAL \
#         --ignore-unfixed --ignorefile /dev/null probe-api
#
# If the second command starts returning findings, do NOT add them here — the
# upgrade has stopped working and that is the bug.
#
# Entries are global rather than per-image on purpose: the two runtime bases
# shared 7 of the original 11, so per-image files would duplicate and drift.
#
# ── What the 2026-08-12 pass changed ─────────────────────────────────────────
#
# All measurements from Trivy 0.72.0 — the pinned version — run against the
# actual images, not inferred from advisory text.
#
#   deploy/docs    caddy:2.10-alpine -> 2.11.4-alpine, digest-pinned, plus an
#                  `apk upgrade`. 128 unique CVEs -> 10; 6 CRITICAL -> 0;
#                  65 HIGH -> 6; gate-blocking 11 -> 0.
#
#   deploy/api     oven/bun:1.3.13 UNCHANGED (engines.bun holds it there — the
#   deploy/web     #2811 --isolate TLA regression), plus an `apt-get upgrade`.
#                  107 unique CVEs -> 75; 6 CRITICAL -> 4; 29 HIGH -> 18;
#                  gate-blocking 7 -> 0.
#
# The baseline went 11 -> 10 entries, and the 10 are NOT a subset of the 11 —
# the caddy bump retired 4 Alpine findings and introduced 2 curl ones the older
# base did not have. Do not diff the counts and assume continuity.
#
# ⚠️ The `apk upgrade` / `apt-get upgrade` is the load-bearing half, NOT the
# base-image bump. Two things this measured that reading advisories would not:
#
#   - `apk add --no-cache curl` is a SILENT NO-OP when the base already has
#     curl at a lower version. caddy:2.11.4-alpine ships curl 8.19.0-r0 and the
#     fix for CVE-2026-5773 / CVE-2026-6276 is 8.20.0-r0, which sits in the
#     v3.23/main repo the image is already pointed at. Without the explicit
#     upgrade the bump ADDS two gate-blocking CVEs instead of clearing them.
#
#   - Bumping oven/bun to 1.3.14 produces the IDENTICAL 75/4/0 as staying on
#     1.3.13 with the upgrade. The fixes are in the security repo, not the base
#     image, so there was never a security argument for moving that pin — which
#     matters, because moving it would have broken the isolated test runner.
#
# ── If a NEW entry is ever needed ─────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Prefer fixing the image. A baseline entry is correct only when the distro has
# published no fixed package (Trivy already filters those with --ignore-unfixed,
# so this is rarer than it looks) or when the fix is unreachable from the pinned
# repo snapshot. Always give it an `exp:` date — the expiry is the forcing
# function; once passed, Trivy stops honouring it and the gate goes red until it
# is fixed or consciously re-dated. Do not bulk-extend dates — re-scan first.

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# oven/bun:1.3.13 (Debian trixie) — runtime base for deploy/api, deploy/web,
# packages/sandbox-sidecar, examples/docker and the create-atlas docker
# templates. All eight are cleared by the `apt-get upgrade` in every runtime
# stage; they persist only in the bare base, which is what base-images scans.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# HIGH     libssl3t64 / openssl-provider-legacy  3.5.5-1~deb13u1 -> u2
CVE-2026-28387 exp:2026-11-01
# HIGH     libssl3t64 / openssl-provider-legacy
CVE-2026-28388 exp:2026-11-01
# HIGH     libssl3t64 / openssl-provider-legacy
CVE-2026-28389 exp:2026-11-01
# HIGH     libssl3t64 / openssl-provider-legacy
CVE-2026-28390 exp:2026-11-01
# CRITICAL libssl3t64 / openssl-provider-legacy
CVE-2026-31789 exp:2026-11-01
# HIGH     libssl3t64 / openssl-provider-legacy  -> 3.5.6-1~deb13u2
CVE-2026-45447 exp:2026-11-01
# HIGH     libcap2  1:2.75-10+b8 -> 1:2.75-10+deb13u1
CVE-2026-4878 exp:2026-11-01
# HIGH     util-linux and its eight siblings — bsdutils, libblkid1, libmount1,
#          libsmartcols1, libuuid1, liblastlog2-2, mount, login
#          2.41-5 -> 2.41.5-0+deb13u1
# Disclosed after the 2026-08-12 pass, so it is the first entry added since. It
# reddened the base-images leg of every PR while main stayed green on a run
# predating the advisory — the gate working as designed, on debt no PR
# introduced.
CVE-2026-53615 exp:2026-11-01

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# caddy:2.11.4-alpine — runtime base for deploy/docs only. Cleared by the
# `apk upgrade` in that runner stage.
#
# ⚠️ The two curl entries are NEW with the 2.11.4 bump and did not exist under
# 2.10-alpine. The base ships curl 8.19.0-r0 pre-installed, so the runner's
# `apk add --no-cache curl` is a silent no-op — only the explicit upgrade
# reaches the 8.20.0-r0 in v3.23/main that fixes them. If that upgrade is ever
# removed, these two ship.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# HIGH     c-ares  1.34.6-r0 -> 1.34.8-r0
CVE-2026-33630 exp:2026-11-01
# HIGH     curl / libcurl  8.19.0-r0 -> 8.20.0-r0
CVE-2026-5773 exp:2026-11-01
# HIGH     curl / libcurl  8.19.0-r0 -> 8.20.0-r0
CVE-2026-6276 exp:2026-11-01
